- Past Confirmed Case [Gorlin_Syndrome]:
  Findings: This composite image presents a multifocal, systemic dermatological condition with characteristic cutaneous and skeletal manifestations. Panel A displays hyperkeratotic, thickened, and fissured skin on the hands, consistent with chronic eczema or lichenification. Panel B reveals multiple small, erythematous papules and plaques within the external auditory canal and surrounding auricular skin, suggesting localized inflammatory or infectious dermatitis. Panel C shows diffuse erythema and fine scaling on the palm, indicative of a generalized inflammatory process such as psoriasis or contact dermatitis. Panel D, a dental radiograph, reveals multiple radiolucent lesions in the mandibular region, suggestive of osteomyelitis or developmental anomalies. Panel E, a skull X-ray, demonstrates generalized osteopenia and possible skull deformities, consistent with skeletal dysplasia or metabolic bone disease. Panel F depicts a large, well-demarcated, hypopigmented plaque on the scalp with surrounding hyperpigmented macules and scattered nodules, resembling a chronic inflammatory or neoplastic process such as a giant condyloma or scleroderma. Panel G shows numerous erythematous, crusted papules and nodules distributed across the trunk and back, with some areas showing central ulceration and surrounding erythema, indicative of a severe, possibly infectious or autoimmune dermatosis. Collectively, these findings suggest a complex, systemic disorder with cutaneous and skeletal involvement, potentially representing a rare genetic or autoimmune condition such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, scleroderma, or a severe form of psoriasis with associated osteopenia.
