This rare aggressive neuroendocrine skin cancer is associated with pathogenic factors such as UV exposure, immunosuppression, and/or Merkel cell polyomavirus. It presents as a rapidly growing bluish red or skin-coloured nodule, usually on a sun-exposed site, and spreads quickly to the lymph nodes, lungs, liver, or bone.  This rare aggressive neuroendocrine skin cancer is associated with pathogenic factors such as UV exposure, immunosuppression, and/or Merkel cell polyomavirus. It presents as a rapidly growing bluish red or skin-coloured nodule, usually on a sun-exposed site, and spreads quickly to the lymph nodes, lungs, liver, or bone.

