[Handbook: Blue_Rubber_Bleb_Nevus_Syndrome]
Bluish purple, non-blanching (purpuric) macules, papules, and/or nodules are produced by extramedullary erythropoiesis in the dermis. Usually involves the head, neck, and trunk. Causes include:  Bluish purple, non-blanching (purpuric) macules, papules, and/or nodules are produced by extramedullary erythropoiesis in the dermis. Usually involves the head, neck, and trunk. Causes include:  •    Congenital infections, e.g. toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus (commonest), herpes simplex (‘TORCH’). Haematological disease, e.g. haemolytic disease of the newborn, hereditary spherocytosis, twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Malignancy/proliferative diseases, e.g. leukaemia, neuroblastoma, congenital rhabdomyosarcoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis.  •    Congenital infections, e.g. toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus (commonest), herpes simplex (‘TORCH’). Haematological disease, e.g. haemolytic disease of the newborn, hereditary spherocytosis, twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Malignancy/proliferative diseases, e.g. leukaemia, neuroblastoma, congenital rhabdomyosarcoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis.  Investigate to determine the cause (including skin biopsy) (see EBox 33.4, p. 649), and manage the underlying condition.  Investigate to determine the cause (including skin biopsy) (see EBox 33.4, p. 649), and manage the underlying condition.
