[Handbook: Blue_Rubber_Bleb_Nevus_Syndrome]
Bluish purple, non-blanching (purpuric) macules, papules, and/or nodules are produced by extramedullary erythropoiesis in the dermis. Usually involves the head, neck, and trunk. Causes include:  Bluish purple, non-blanching (purpuric) macules, papules, and/or nodules are produced by extramedullary erythropoiesis in the dermis. Usually involves the head, neck, and trunk. Causes include:  •    Congenital infections, e.g. toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus (commonest), herpes simplex (‘TORCH’). Haematological disease, e.g. haemolytic disease of the newborn, hereditary spherocytosis, twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Malignancy/proliferative diseases, e.g. leukaemia, neuroblastoma, congenital rhabdomyosarcoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis.  •    Congenital infections, e.g. toxoplasmosis, other, rubella, cytomegalovirus (commonest), herpes simplex (‘TORCH’). Haematological disease, e.g. haemolytic disease of the newborn, hereditary spherocytosis, twin-twin transfusion syndrome. Malignancy/proliferative diseases, e.g. leukaemia, neuroblastoma, congenital rhabdomyosarcoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis.  Investigate to determine the cause (including skin biopsy) (see EBox 33.4, p. 649), and manage the underlying condition.  Investigate to determine the cause (including skin biopsy) (see EBox 33.4, p. 649), and manage the underlying condition.
A combination of a linear sebaceous naevus in the lines of Blaschko and a speckled lentiginous melanocytic naevus in a checkerboard pattern (the pigmented patches do not cross the midline).  A combination of a linear sebaceous naevus in the lines of Blaschko and a speckled lentiginous melanocytic naevus in a checkerboard pattern (the pigmented patches do not cross the midline).  Associated problems may include: Mild intellectual disability and seizures. Segmental sensory abnormalities and segmental hyperhidrosis on the same side as the speckled lentiginous melanocytic naevus. Deafness. •     Ptosis and strabismus. •    Hemiatrophy with muscle weakness.  Associated problems may include: Mild intellectual disability and seizures. Segmental sensory abnormalities and segmental hyperhidrosis on the same side as the speckled lentiginous melanocytic naevus. Deafness. •     Ptosis and strabismus. •    Hemiatrophy with muscle weakness.

[Handbook: Dermatofibrosarcoma_Protuberans_Images]
A slow-growing, locally recurrent cutaneous soft tissue sarcoma caused by a chromosomal translocation t(17;22)(q22;q13) which results in a COL1A1-PDGFB fusion gene. Presents as an asymptomatic 'scar' or a violaceous, reddish brown, or skin-coloured nodule or plaque, which feels rubbery or firm. Has an infiltrative pattern, often with considerable subclinical and asymmetrical extension. Treatment:  surgical excision $(1-3mathtt{c m}$ peripheral margins) or Mohs micrographic surgery. Rarely metastasizes $(<5%)$ .  A slow-growing, locally recurrent cutaneous soft tissue sarcoma caused by a chromosomal translocation t(17;22)(q22;q13) which results in a COL1A1-PDGFB fusion gene. Presents as an asymptomatic 'scar' or a violaceous, reddish brown, or skin-coloured nodule or plaque, which feels rubbery or firm. Has an infiltrative pattern, often with considerable subclinical and asymmetrical extension. Treatment:  surgical excision $(1-3\mathtt{c m}$ peripheral margins) or Mohs micrographic surgery. Rarely metastasizes $(<5\%)$ .
•    Granuloma annulare: colour may be similar to morphoea, but look for a raised beaded margin in an annular configuration (see E Box 22.3, p. 469). •  Erythema migrans (see E p. 148). Subtle erythema but no thickening. •   Extragenital lichen sclerosus: resembles plaque morphoea, but the surface is slightly hyperkeratotic with follicular plugs. Lichen sclerosus (genital and extragenital) may occur with morphoea (see $spadesuit$ pp. 288–9). · Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans: a slow-growing fibrosing tumour that may present as a thickened plaque (see E p. 360).  •    Granuloma annulare: colour may be similar to morphoea, but look for a raised beaded margin in an annular configuration (see E Box 22.3, p. 469). •  Erythema migrans (see E p. 148). Subtle erythema but no thickening. •   Extragenital lichen sclerosus: resembles plaque morphoea, but the surface is slightly hyperkeratotic with follicular plugs. Lichen sclerosus (genital and extragenital) may occur with morphoea (see $\spadesuit$ pp. 288–9). · Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans: a slow-growing fibrosing tumour that may present as a thickened plaque (see E p. 360).

